{"agent":"fleet-verifybugs","data":{"by":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"`tree_provides` reports \"the test is not in the base's test files\" for every non-`passed` outcome of the collection probe, not just for a genuinely absent test. Concretely: base tree contains `tests/test_widget.py` with `def test_widget` (so the old check returned True and the tests were actually run at base), but the module does `import <something>` that fails to collect at the base commit — e.g. the fix adds/renames a symbol the test imports at module level, or a test-only dependency (`pytest-asyncio`, `httpx`, …) is not installed in the environment running ddflow. pytest then exits 2/4 with a collection error, `run_command_gate` yields a non-`passed` outcome, `tree_provides` returns False, and `verify_test_only_fix` sets `restored = \"not run (the test is not in the base's test files)\"`. That message is false (the base file plainly defines the test, as the deleted `_exists_at_base` proved) and the restored-test-files check is skipped again, so for any test pytest cannot *collect* the gate silently degrades to the mutation-only path — the bug this change claims to close. The pre-check's `is_file()` test only rules out the missing-file case; it cannot distinguish \"absent\" from \"collect failed\". The same conflation happens on exit code 5 (zero items collected, e.g. a spec that is a bare file path or a test parametrized to zero cases): the base file exists but `outcome == \"passed\"` is False. No `_ev`/reason is inspected, so this is indistinguishable from real absence at the call site.","digest":"dbd8500089808b68","location":"ddflow/services/gates/regression_collect.py:32-35 (return outcome == \"passed\")","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/gates/regression_collect.py:32-35 (return outcome == \"passed\")"},{"chunk":2,"detail":"The added test never drives the path the bug report and the change's own intent describe. Its own comment admits the reason: \"The gate itself, not `bug fixed`: its static name check (`_unresolved_tests`) does not see a re-export either, a separate defect filed on its own.\" So it deliberately calls the internal service function `G.verify_regression_test(repo, Config.load(repo), tree=wt, base=\"main\", tests=[\"tests/test_v.py::test_v\"])` with the node id already resolved, side-stepping the CLI/gate name-resolution step. Concrete repro: in the exact repository this test builds, a user closes the bug with the documented command, `ddflow bug fixed B1 --regression-test tests/test_v.py::test_v` (or `ddflow complete fix-B1 --regression-test ...`), and the gate reaches `_unresolved_tests` first. Per the test's own comment that check also fails to see a name that is only imported/re-exported, so the re-exported-test scenario is still rejected in the user-facing flow — i.e. the reported bug (the restored-test-files verification being skipped for re-exported tests) is not reproducible-then-fixed through the actual workflow; only one internal helper is. The change therefore does not satisfy the hard ddflow requirement that the bug-closing change carry a regression test that reproduces the reported bug through the workflow it belongs to [D-rules-hierarchy]. Making the test call the CLI (or extending the fix so `_unresolved_tests` also resolves collected node ids) is what would demonstrate the bug as reported is actually gone.","digest":"bfbdeb1299828c85","location":"tests/test_regression_testfix.py:242 (test_a_test_the_base_re_exports_is_seen_as_existing_there)","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"tests/test_regression_testfix.py:242 (test_a_test_the_base_re_exports_is_seen_as_existing_there)"}],"chunks_total":2,"coverage":"2/2 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":5849,"diff_sha":"88afe1ae2aa199fa9cab1ae602e0eb73dbfb48bd75e5e95dd1a880da42a03f17","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in [REDACTED:path]","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"elapsed_s":336.4,"family":"deepseek","findings":2,"max_chunk_chars":5000,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","output_digest":"e38b2fbf95f0b361","output_file":".ddflow/local/reviews/fix-B9f3ae012b9.critic.lan-deepseek-v4.1-flash.20261016T131410Z-c9e01d54.jsonl","reason":"","review_kind":"full","reviewed":[1,2],"reviewed_head":"dd835bc97c5d623b491043f4824067b3ca71b8c2","reviewer":"lan-deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewer_digest":"d34b8af1b4a590b5","round":1,"rounds":1,"status":"REVIEWED","titles":["ddflow/services/gates/regression_collect.py:32-35 (return outcome == \"passed\")","tests/test_regression_testfix.py:242 (test_a_test_the_base_re_exports_is_seen_as_existing_there)"],"tree_sha":"dd835bc97c5d+clean","unreviewed":[],"waves":1},"gate":"critic","reason":"2 finding(s)"},"id":"e35d3ea80c98f501404836b63","kind":"gate.failed","lamport":69444,"schema":1,"subject":"fix-B9f3ae012b9","ts":"2026-10-16T13:19:47.096196Z"}
{"agent":"fleet-verifybugs","data":{"by":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","evidence":{"chunk_findings":[{"chunk":1,"detail":"`tree_provides` collapses every non-zero pytest exit status into \"the named test is not present in the base tree\" (`return outcome == \"passed\"`, line 33), but pytest distinguishes *node id not found* (exit 4) from a *collection error in the target file* (exit 2, e.g. ImportError/SyntaxError raised while importing the module or its `conftest.py`, missing plugin, etc.). The replacement of `_exists_at_base` therefore changes the answer for inputs the old text scan handled correctly. Concrete input: at base, `tests/test_api.py` contains a top-level `from mymod import new_helper` (the symbol the fix adds) and `def test_new_helper(): ...`. The old `_exists_at_base` read the file and matched the literal `def test_new_helper`, returning True, so the restored-test-files check ran and observed the base failure. With this change, `pytest --collect-only -q` aborts with a collection error and exit code 2, `tree_provides` returns False, and `verify_test_only_fix` records `restored = \"not run (the test is not in the base's test files)\"` — the very silent skip that bug B9f3ae012b9 is about, for exactly the class of regression tests that import the fixed API at module scope. The same applies to any configured command that is not pytest (the code unconditionally appends `--collect-only -q` to `configured`, so a `go test`/`unittest`/wrapper command always fails and the check is permanently disabled). The function needs to distinguish \"id genuinely absent\" from \"runner/collection failed\", not treat both as absent.","digest":"4bfb97168aa44363","location":"ddflow/services/gates/regression_collect.py::tree_provides","severity":"MEDIUM","title":"ddflow/services/gates/regression_collect.py::tree_provides"}],"chunks_total":2,"coverage":"2/2 chunk(s) reviewed","delta_rounds":0,"diff_chars":5849,"diff_sha":"88afe1ae2aa199fa9cab1ae602e0eb73dbfb48bd75e5e95dd1a880da42a03f17","diff_source":"main..HEAD + tracked working-tree changes in [REDACTED:path]","diff_stat":{"deletions":0,"files":0,"insertions":0,"untracked":0},"elapsed_s":355.1,"family":"deepseek","findings":1,"max_chunk_chars":5000,"model":"deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash","output_digest":"9e50cbf8d6f9967d","output_file":".ddflow/local/reviews/fix-B9f3ae012b9.rubber_duck.lan-deepseek-v4.1-flash.20261016T131408Z-4f941b8f.jsonl","reason":"","review_kind":"full","reviewed":[1,2],"reviewed_head":"dd835bc97c5d623b491043f4824067b3ca71b8c2","reviewer":"lan-deepseek-v4.1-flash","reviewer_digest":"d34b8af1b4a590b5","round":1,"rounds":1,"status":"REVIEWED","titles":["ddflow/services/gates/regression_collect.py::tree_provides"],"tree_sha":"dd835bc97c5d+clean","unreviewed":[],"waves":1},"gate":"rubber_duck","reason":"1 finding(s)"},"id":"e1db74a1df87e1edc74fced47","kind":"gate.failed","lamport":69445,"schema":1,"subject":"fix-B9f3ae012b9","ts":"2026-10-16T13:20:04.274074Z"}
